Unbounded metadata command cache enables memory DoS
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/cf13c24300f88191bffa1907eef1693b?repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sev=
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: resolved

# Resolution
This finding was valid for commit `e4dbc96`.

It was resolved in commit `410d5ff` (`storage: harden metadata command log
recovery`). That change removed the process-lifetime
`LocalClusterRuntimeState.applied_metadata_commands` cache and the
`mark_metadata_command_applied` insertion path that caused heap growth once per
metadata command per acting node.

Replica idempotence and conflict detection now read from each PG's durable
SQLite `metadata_command_log` and `metadata_command_replica_state` instead of
an unbounded in-memory `HashMap`. Metadata command application and durable log
recording are performed in one SQLite transaction through
`PgStore::apply_metadata_command_and_record`, so successful command application
does not retain per-command process heap state for later duplicate detection.

Follow-up: the fix changes the risk from unbounded process heap growth to
normal durable command-log growth on disk. Metadata command-log retention and
compaction are tracked in `plans/multihost-transition-plan.md`: Phase 6a now
defines the compaction policy and Phase 9 implements retention/compaction once
peering and repair semantics are in place. That plan update was recorded in
commit `9389766` (`Track metadata command log compaction`).

