# Summary
Introduced: the commit adds applied_metadata_commands as an unbounded runtime cache and inserts into it after every successful command application. Existing HTTP-accessible metadata mutation paths can generate arbitrarily many metadata commands, so the new cache creates attacker-amplified process memory growth.
The new replica-acceptance hardening records every applied metadata command in LocalClusterRuntimeState.applied_metadata_commands, keyed by target node, PG, and monotonically increasing log index. Each successful metadata command application calls mark_metadata_command_applied(), which inserts a new entry, but there is no eviction, compaction, per-PG high-water mark, or durable replay window limiting this map. A client that can perform metadata-mutating S3 operations can repeatedly issue low-payload requests, such as PutObjectTagging on the same object, causing this HashMap to grow for the lifetime of the process even when persistent object metadata stays bounded. This creates an introduced remote denial-of-service vector via heap exhaustion. The cache should be bounded, replaced with compact per-replica log progress plus a limited retry window, or pruned after commands can no longer be retried.

