## Report
Validated the finding. Key code evidence: LocalClusterRuntimeState contains applied_metadata_commands as HashMap<(NodeId, PgId, u64), u64> at crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:211-217. mark_metadata_command_applied inserts by target node, PG, and log index at local.rs:356-368 with no eviction in the inspected changed code. Every successful command application calls it at crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:631-636. PutObjectTagging is HTTP exposed at crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:2283-2300, flows through coordinator put_object_tags at crates/server-core/src/coordinator/object_metadata.rs:269-327, then StorageCluster::put_object_tags_if at crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:2261-2276. The metadata update path allocates a fresh log index at request_ops.rs:2146-2151 and applies the new command at request_ops.rs:2247-2256. I added targeted validation tests. The real put-object-tags path on the same object showed exact linear cache growth: with ARGMIN_VALIDATION_TAG_UPDATES=100, output was `VALIDATION_CACHE_GROWTH baseline=6 after=306 growth=300 expected_growth=300 pg_id=1`, i.e. 100 requests * 3 acting nodes. A larger run with 5000 updates produced `growth=15000`. This confirms attacker-controllable repeated low-payload metadata mutations grow the process-local cache for each command/node even after pending commands clear. Direct heap-exhaustion was reproduced against the actual vulnerable cache by running the synthetic cache test under a 32MB virtual-memory limit: `memory allocation of 13107216 bytes failed`, exit code 6. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable (`bash: valgrind: command not found`). Debugger validation used lldb: breakpoint at request_ops.rs:2151 showed the chain `put_object_tags_if -> put_object_metadata_if -> new_put_object_metadata_command -> next_metadata_command_log_index`; breakpoint at local.rs:364 showed `mark_metadata_command_applied -> apply_metadata_command_to_acting_set_from_origin` at request_ops.rs:636.

